„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^For whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙâThen said he also to him that bade him, When thou makest a dinner or a supper, call not thy friends, nor thy brethren, neither thy kinsmen, nor thy rich neighbours; lest they also bid thee again, and a recompence be made thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMBut when thou makest a feast, call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzand thou shalt be blessed; for they cannot recompense thee: for thou shalt be recompensed at the resurrection of the just.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd when one of them that sat at meat with him heard these things, he said unto him, Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHThen said he unto him, A certain man made a great supper, and bade many:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhand sent his servant at supper time to say to them that were bidden, Come; for all things are now ready.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙªAnd they all with one consent began to make excuse. The first said unto him, I have bought a piece of ground, and I must needs go and see it: I pray thee have me excused.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgAnd another said, I have bought five yoke of oxen, and I go to prove them: I pray thee have me excused.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙEAnd another said, I have married a wife, and therefore I cannot come.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ÷So that servant came, and shewed his lord these things. Then the master of the house being angry said to his servant, Go out quickly into the streets and lanes of the city, and bring in hither the poor, and the maimed, and the halt, and the blind.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUAnd the servant said, Lord, it is done as thou hast commanded, and yet there is room.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd the lord said unto the servant, Go out into the highways and hedges, and compel them to come in, that my house may be filled.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVFor I say unto you, That none of those men which were bidden shall taste of my supper.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙLAnd there went great multitudes with him: and he turned, and said unto them,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPAnd whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…For which of you, intending to build a tower, sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost, whether he have sufficient to finish it?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙrLest haply, after he hath laid the foundation, and is not able to finish it, all that behold it begin to mock him,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ<saying, This man began to build, and was not able to finish.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse!¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ»Or what king, going to make war against another king, sitteth not down first, and consulteth whether he be able with ten thousand to meet him that cometh against him with twenty thousand?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkOr else, while the other is yet a great way off, he sendeth an ambassage, and desireth conditions of peace.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbSo likewise, whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple.¦layout ªlast_verse!…¤book)§chapter«first_verse"ªlast_verse#¦verses’„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSSalt is good: but if the salt have lost his savour, wherewith shall it be seasoned?¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwIt is neither fit for the land, nor yet for the dunghill; but men cast it out. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.¦layout ªlast_verse#